# The rule: `move`, as inputs, clauses and bindings.
#
# **A clause says which relation and what to do with it; a binding says which column takes which
# input.** They are two relations because a clause cannot carry `residency`'s columns - those
# change with the relation it points at, and a relation's columns do not change.
#
# **A command names the row it acts on by its id.** `it` is the residency being moved, and the
# other three inputs are what the world already knows about it - stated by the command and checked
# against the world by the first clause, so none of them can be lied about. **That is what the
# foundation style costs a caller**, and it is what the user-facing style exists to hide.
#
# **`require` and `remove` are patterns; `add` is a whole row.** The adjacency clause names no id
# because it asks whether *any* road runs that way. The add clause names every column, including
# the `id` the new row will be known by - which is why `it` is an input at all: the residency keeps
# its identity across the move rather than becoming a different row.

{rule id:1 name:move}

{input id:1 rule:move seq:1 name:it of:residency}
{input id:2 rule:move seq:2 name:what of:thing}
{input id:3 rule:move seq:3 name:from of:territory}
{input id:4 rule:move seq:4 name:to of:territory}

{clause id:1 rule:move seq:1 role:require relation:residency name:clause-1}
{clause id:2 rule:move seq:2 role:require relation:adjacency name:clause-2}
{clause id:3 rule:move seq:3 role:remove relation:residency name:clause-3}
{clause id:4 rule:move seq:4 role:add relation:residency name:clause-4}

{binding id:1 clause:clause-1 column:44 input:it}
{binding id:2 clause:clause-1 column:45 input:what}
{binding id:3 clause:clause-1 column:46 input:from}

{binding id:4 clause:clause-2 column:42 input:from}
{binding id:5 clause:clause-2 column:43 input:to}

{binding id:6 clause:clause-3 column:44 input:it}
{binding id:7 clause:clause-3 column:45 input:what}
{binding id:8 clause:clause-3 column:46 input:from}

{binding id:9 clause:clause-4 column:44 input:it}
{binding id:10 clause:clause-4 column:45 input:what}
{binding id:11 clause:clause-4 column:46 input:to}
